Article 21.

Education as referred to in Article 20 is directed at:

a. development of the child's attitude and personality abilities, talents, mental and physical abilities to achieve their optimal potential;
b. development of respect for human rights and fundamental freedoms;
c. development of respect for parents, cultural identity, language and their own values, national values where the child resides, from where the child originates, and civilizations that differ from their own civilization;
d. preparation of children for a responsible life; and

e. development of respect and love for the environment.

Article 27

(1) The government in providing care and maintenance is obliged to strive for and assist children, so that children can:

a. participate;
b. freely express opinions and think in accordance with their conscience and religion;
c. freely receive oral or written information in accordance with the child's age and development stage;
d. freely associate and assemble;
e. freely rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts; and

f. obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements.

2. The efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are adjusted to age, environment, and the child's level of ability, so as not to hinder and disturb the child's development.

Article 31

Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 3O consist of:

a. children who become refugees;
b. children who are victims of unrest;
c. children who are victims of natural disasters; and

d. children in armed conflict situations.

Article 35

1. Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 3O includes children in conflict with the law and children who are victims of criminal acts, which is the obligation and responsibility of the government and society.
2. Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through:
a. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
b. provision of special child assistance officers from an early stage;
c. provision of special facilities and infrastructure;
d. imposition of appropriate sanctions for the best interests of the child;
e. continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law;
f. provision of guarantees to maintain relationships with parents or family; and
g. protection from identity disclosure through the mass media and to avoid labeling.
3. Special protection for children who are victims of criminal acts as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through:
a. rehabilitation efforts, both in institutions and outside institutions;
b. protection efforts from identity disclosure through the mass media and to avoid labeling;
c. provision of safety guarantees for victim witnesses and expert witnesses, whether physical, mental, or social; and
d. provision of accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments.

Article 43

Persons with Disabilities have the right to:

a. life;
b. freedom from stigma;
c. privacy;
d. justice and legal protection;
e. education;
f. employment, entrepreneurship, and cooperatives;
g. health;
h. politics;
i. religion;
j. sports;
k. culture and tourism;
l. social welfare;
m. accessibility;
n. Public services;
o. protection from disasters;
p. habilitation and rehabilitation;
q. concessions;
r. data collection;

s. live independently and be included in society;
t. express themselves, communicate, and obtain information;
u. freedom of movement and citizenship; and
v. freedom from acts of Discrimination, neglect, torture, and exploitation.

Article 44

In addition to the rights of Persons with Disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1), women with disabilities have the right to:

a. reproductive health;
b. accept or reject the use of contraceptives;
c. obtain greater Protection from multiple Discrimination;
d. obtain greater Protection from acts of violence, including sexual violence and exploitation.

Article 45

In addition to the rights of Persons with Disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1), children with disabilities have the right to:

a. obtain special Protection from Discrimination, neglect, harassment, exploitation, as well as sexual violence and crimes;
b. obtain family care and upbringing or substitute family for optimal growth and development;
c. have their interests protected in decision-making;
d. treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of the child;
e. fulfillment of special needs;
f. equal treatment with other children to achieve social integration and individual development; and
g. obtain social assistance.

Article 46

The right to life for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to:

a. respect for integrity;
b. not be deprived of life;
c. obtain care and upbringing that ensures their survival;
d. freedom from neglect, shackling, confinement, and isolation;
e. freedom from threats and various forms of exploitation; and
f. freedom from torture, other cruel, inhuman, and degrading treatment and punishment.

Article 48

The right to privacy for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to:

a. be recognized as a human person who can demand and obtain equal treatment and Protection in accordance with human dignity in public;
b. form a family and continue descendants through a valid marriage;
c. respect for home and family;
d. obtain Protection of personal and family life; and
e. have the confidentiality of personal data, correspondence, and other forms of personal communication protected, including health data and information.

Article 49

The right to justice and legal protection for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to:

a. equal treatment before the law;
b. be recognized as a legal subject;
c. own and inherit movable or immovable property;
d. control financial matters or appoint a person to represent their interests in financial matters;
e. obtain access to banking and non-banking services;
f. obtain provision of Accessibility in judicial services;
24g. Protection from all pressure, violence, persecution, discrimination, and/or deprivation or seizure of property rights;

choose and appoint a person to represent their interests in the matter of
h. civil matters inside and outside the court; and protected their intellectual property rights.

Article 5O

The right to education for Persons with Disabilities includes the right to:

a. obtain quality education at educational units in all types, pathways, and levels of education in an inclusive and special manner;

b. have Equal Opportunity to become an educator or educational personnel at educational units in all types, pathways, and levels of education;

c. have Equal Opportunity as a provider of quality education at educational units in all types, pathways, and levels of education; and

d. obtain Reasonable Accommodation as students.

Article 83 states: Any person who traffics, sells, or kidnaps a child for themselves or for sale, shall be punished with imprisonment for a maximum of 15 (fifteen) years and a minimum of 3 (three) years and a fine of at most Rp. 300,000,000,- (three hundred million rupiah) and at least Rp. 60,000,000,- (sixty million rupiah).

These three Articles are very reformative Articles when compared with the provisions in the Criminal Code, which in Articles 28L to 296 regulate provisions, where the heaviest criminal threat is Article 295, namely 12 years. When compared, the Child Protection Law has been quite severe in establishing criminal threats and fines as regulated in Article 81 to Article 83.

The problem is, even though the Law has established heavy threats or legal sanctions, there are still Law Enforcement Officials who have not optimally implemented them, so there is still a long way to go, between law in book and law in action. In cases as regulated in Article 81 to Article 83, Law Enforcement Officials often conduct investigation and inquiry processes that are quite heavy and exhausting for child victims of violence, but in prosecution and judges' verdicts they are still light so they are not in accordance with justice.

Violence against women and children also occurs frequently within the household scope, even though we have had a law for 13 years, namely Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (KDRT), but nowadays domestic violence is increasing in number. For the prevention and handling of domestic violence in Musi Rawas Regency, the Office of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of Musi Rawas Regency has established an Integrated Service Center for the Protection of Women and Children (P2TP2A) of Musi Rawas Regency, based on the Decree of the Regent of Musi Rawas Number 703/KPTS/KPP/2015, namely concerning the Establishment of the Integrated Service Center for Women's Empowerment and Children (P2TP2A) of Musi Rawas Regency. The Integrated Service Center for Women's Empowerment and Children (P2TP2A) serves as a place for reporting, handling, rehabilitation and reintegration of victims of violence. If there is domestic violence or other violence affecting women and children, it must be reported to the P2TP2A, but the existence of P2TP2A is not yet widely known by the community.

Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, in essence is to realize a sakinah, mawadah, and warohmah household, this can be read from the objectives of eliminating domestic violence, namely: (a) preventing all forms of domestic violence (KDRT); (b) protecting victims of domestic violence, (c) prosecuting perpetrators of domestic violence and (d) maintaining the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household. So that we better understand the prohibition of domestic violence, important matters from Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning domestic violence will be briefly described as follows: Types of domestic violence, include:

a. physical violence, is an act that results in (1) pain, (2) falling ill, or (3) serious injury;

b. psychological violence, is an act that results in (1) fear, (2) loss of self-confidence, (3) loss of ability to act, (4) feeling of helplessness, (5) and/or severe psychological suffering to a person;

c. sexual violence includes: (1) forced sexual relations committed against a person including a child, who resides within the household scope, (2) forced sexual relations against one person within the household scope with another person including a child, for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes;

d. household neglect, includes (1) neglecting a person including a child within the household scope, whereas according to the law applicable to them or because of an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person or child, (2) resulting in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

